The problem was, I’d only prepared a few acoustic numbers. So, I scrambled. I brought my trusty mandolin, a ukulele I’d never really gigged with, and a surprisingly loud kazoo I’d bought on a whim at a novelty shop. The ukulele was surprisingly decent, but the mandolin was a bit out of tune, and the kazoo… well, the kazoo was an adventure.

The room was packed with folks in cardigans, all very earnest about preserving the town's founding documents. I started with some mandolin tunes, which went over okay, if a little politely. Then I switched to the ukulele. I was attempting a rather complex fingerpicking piece when disaster struck. A moth, a truly enormous, fuzzy specimen, decided my head was the perfect landing strip. I yelped, dropped my pick, and nearly fell off my rickety stool. The audience gasped, then a few people chuckled.

Agnes, bless her, tried to shoo the moth away with a hymn book, which only agitated it further. It fluttered around my face like a tiny, winged heckler. In a moment of pure, unadulterated panic, I grabbed the kazoo. I figured if I was going to make a fool of myself, I might as well go all in. I blew into it with all my might, producing a sound that was less music and more a distressed goose being strangled by a vacuum cleaner.

To my utter astonishment, the audience erupted in laughter. Not polite chuckles, but full-on belly laughs. The moth, apparently startled by the sonic assault, flew away. I, still clutching the kazoo, looked out at the sea of smiling faces and, between gasps of laughter, managed to get out a few more "musical" notes. The rest of the set was a blur of ukulele, mandolin, and increasingly absurd kazoo solos. By the end, they were cheering. The local newspaper even ran a small article with the headline, "Kazoo Virtuoso Saves Historical Fundraiser." I’m still not sure if they were being serious.

